# Line-ending policy for artifacts whose BYTES are a contract.
#
# Why this file exists (found the hard way, 2026-08-18, on the meter):
# `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows checks the corpus out as CRLF while the
# committed blob is LF. Every Stage A schedule pins `corpus_sha256` in its
# provenance, and those hashes were generated against the Windows working-tree
# form -- so replay worked on Windows and failed on Linux with "corpus drift
# detected". The frozen workload was only reproducible on the platform that
# froze it, which defeats the point of freezing it.
#
# `-text` means git never translates line endings in either direction: the
# working tree gets the blob's bytes verbatim on every platform. For a file
# whose SHA-256 is a reproducibility contract, that is the only safe setting.

# The pinned prompt corpus -- hashed into every schedule's provenance.
corpus/*.jsonl -text

# Benchmark evidence: raw request logs and TTFT/TPOT sample sidecars promoted
# into benchmarks/evidence/. These are the durable record BASELINE.md cites,
# and they are read back by scripts/compute_point_metrics.py offline.
benchmarks/**/*.jsonl -text

# Promoted evidence -- EVERY file, not a list of extensions. MANIFEST.json
# records a SHA-256 for each promoted artifact, so any file whose bytes git is
# willing to rewrite on checkout breaks its own manifest entry. A hash recorded
# on Windows against CRLF bytes and re-checked on Linux against LF bytes is the
# corpus-drift bug wearing a different extension.
#
# It was written per-extension first (*.json, *.jsonl) and that missed the
# session's .log files, which then failed their manifest check in a fresh
# checkout. The lesson is the rule: in a tree where the bytes ARE the contract,
# the default has to be "never translate", not "never translate the extensions
# I remembered".
#
# Scoped to benchmarks/evidence/ deliberately: it holds only newly promoted
# files, so no already-committed blob is renormalized.
benchmarks/evidence/** -text

# Week 2 redesign artifact formats (R4 README P2). New paths only: the
# historical schedules under benchmarks/schedules/stage_a/ are deliberately
# NOT listed, because adding a rule there would mark every committed blob as
# modified and invite a renormalizing rewrite of evidence that must stay
# byte-identical. Protection goes on the formats that do not exist yet.
#
# `metrics.artifacts.write_json_artifact` is the other half: it stops Python
# translating newlines on write. This stops git translating them on checkout.
benchmarks/workloads/**/*.json -text
benchmarks/schedules/week2_redesign/**/*.json -text
